+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_orig_mnqwy3c7ga3ggnrwmfrtemdegq3gemjtmjrtkmbxgq4tkzi","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Phase 7: Task Dependencies\n2\t\n3\tAdd support for task dependencies, where tasks can wait for other tasks to complete before becoming available.\n4\t\n5\t## Updated Methods\n6\t\n7\t- **`enqueue(queueName, payload, options?)`** - Options can now include:\n8\t  - `dependsOn`: an array of task IDs that must complete before this task becomes available\n9\t\n10\t## New Methods\n11\t\n12\t- **`getDependencies(taskId)`** - Return dependency information for a task, including each dependency's ID and current status.\n13\t\n14\t## Requirements\n15\t\n16\t- A task with dependencies remains blocked (not available for dequeue) until ALL dependencies have `completed` status\n17\t- When a dependency completes (via `complete(taskId)`), the system checks if any dependent tasks are now unblocked\n18\t- Circular dependencies must be detected and throw an error at enqueue time\n19\t  - Direct circular: A depends on B, B depends on A\n20\t  - Indirect circular: A → B → C → A\n21\t- `getDependencies(taskId)` returns an array of `{ taskId, status }` objects\n22\t- Dependency on a non-existent task ID should throw an error\n23\t- A failed dependency does NOT auto-fail the dependent task - it stays blocked\n24\t- Cascading dependencies work: if A depends on B and B depends on C, A is only available after both B and C complete\n25\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bb6276d8-e493-46dd-af72-8571635da619","uuid":"967af831-a10a-41d9-b42d-fa06cf437c03","timestamp":"2026-06-18T06:15:49.616Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/phases/phase-07.md","content":"# Phase 7: Task Dependencies\n\nAdd support for task dependencies, where tasks can wait for other tasks to complete before becoming available.\n\n## Updated Methods\n\n- **`enqueue(queueName, payload, options?)`** - Options can now include:\n  - `dependsOn`: an array of task IDs that must complete before this task becomes available\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`getDependencies(taskId)`** - Return dependency information for a task, including each dependency's ID and current status.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- A task with dependencies remains blocked (not available for dequeue) until ALL dependencies have `completed` status\n- When a dependency completes (via `complete(taskId)`), the system checks if any dependent tasks are now unblocked\n- Circular dependencies must be detected and throw an error at enqueue time\n  - Direct circular: A depends on B, B depends on A\n  - Indirect circular: A → B → C → A\n- `getDependencies(taskId)` returns an array of `{ taskId, status }` objects\n- Dependency on a non-existent task ID should throw an error\n- A failed dependency does NOT auto-fail the dependent task - it stays blocked\n- Cascading dependencies work: if A depends on B and B depends on C, A is only available after both B and C complete\n","numLines":25,"startLine":1,"totalLines":25}}}
+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_orig_mnqwy3c7gfqtgnbsmuzdeyjxmfrdoolcgbsgiyjrhe2dizq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Phase 8: Concurrency Control\n2\t\n3\tAdd per-queue concurrency limits to control how many tasks can be processed simultaneously.\n4\t\n5\t## New Methods\n6\t\n7\t- **`setConcurrency(queueName, limit)`** - Set the maximum number of tasks that can be in `processing` status simultaneously for a queue.\n8\t- **`getConcurrency(queueName)`** - Return concurrency information: `{ current: number, max: number }`.\n9\t\n10\t## Requirements\n11\t\n12\t- `dequeue()` returns `null` if the queue has reached its concurrency limit, even if tasks are available\n13\t- `complete(taskId)` and `fail(taskId)` free up a concurrency slot\n14\t- Default concurrency is unlimited (no limit) until `setConcurrency` is called\n15\t- Each queue has its own independent concurrency limit\n16\t- Concurrency interacts correctly with priorities (highest priority task still chosen, but only if a slot is available)\n17\t- `getConcurrency(queueName)` returns the current number of processing tasks and the configured maximum\n18\t  - For unlimited queues, `max` should be `Infinity` or `0` to indicate no limit\n19\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bb6276d8-e493-46dd-af72-8571635da619","uuid":"821e1883-7db3-4082-b920-f39d23cbb891","timestamp":"2026-06-18T06:15:49.618Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/phases/phase-08.md","content":"# Phase 8: Concurrency Control\n\nAdd per-queue concurrency limits to control how many tasks can be processed simultaneously.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`setConcurrency(queueName, limit)`** - Set the maximum number of tasks that can be in `processing` status simultaneously for a queue.\n- **`getConcurrency(queueName)`** - Return concurrency information: `{ current: number, max: number }`.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- `dequeue()` returns `null` if the queue has reached its concurrency limit, even if tasks are available\n- `complete(taskId)` and `fail(taskId)` free up a concurrency slot\n- Default concurrency is unlimited (no limit) until `setConcurrency` is called\n- Each queue has its own independent concurrency limit\n- Concurrency interacts correctly with priorities (highest priority task still chosen, but only if a slot is available)\n- `getConcurrency(queueName)` returns the current number of processing tasks and the configured maximum\n  - For unlimited queues, `max` should be `Infinity` or `0` to indicate no limit\n","numLines":19,"startLine":1,"totalLines":19}}}
+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_orig_mnqwy3c7gu3wczbumnrwcyztgq3tczrumztdmobsgyzdayy","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Phase 9: Progress and Cancellation\n2\t\n3\tAdd progress tracking and task cancellation support.\n4\t\n5\t## New Methods\n6\t\n7\t- **`updateProgress(taskId, percent, message?)`** - Report progress for a processing task (0-100).\n8\t- **`getProgress(taskId)`** - Return progress info: `{ percent: number, message?: string, updatedAt: Date }`.\n9\t- **`cancel(taskId)`** - Cancel a pending or processing task. Sets status to `'cancelled'`.\n10\t- **`onProgress(taskId, callback)`** - Subscribe to progress updates. Callback receives `{ percent, message, updatedAt }`.\n11\t\n12\t## Requirements\n13\t\n14\t- Progress percentage must be between 0 and 100\n15\t- `updateProgress` fires all registered `onProgress` callbacks for that task\n16\t- `cancel()` works on both `pending` and `processing` tasks\n17\t- A cancelled pending task is removed from the queue (not available for dequeue)\n18\t- A cancelled processing task has its status set to `'cancelled'`\n19\t- Cancelled tasks are NOT retried (even if they have retries remaining)\n20\t- `getProgress(taskId)` returns `null` if no progress has been reported yet\n21\t- The `message` parameter in `updateProgress` is optional\n22\t- `onProgress` returns an unsubscribe function\n23\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bb6276d8-e493-46dd-af72-8571635da619","uuid":"a30b52ae-364e-4ca0-a4c3-8b5fed07736f","timestamp":"2026-06-18T06:15:49.617Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/phases/phase-09.md","content":"# Phase 9: Progress and Cancellation\n\nAdd progress tracking and task cancellation support.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`updateProgress(taskId, percent, message?)`** - Report progress for a processing task (0-100).\n- **`getProgress(taskId)`** - Return progress info: `{ percent: number, message?: string, updatedAt: Date }`.\n- **`cancel(taskId)`** - Cancel a pending or processing task. Sets status to `'cancelled'`.\n- **`onProgress(taskId, callback)`** - Subscribe to progress updates. Callback receives `{ percent, message, updatedAt }`.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Progress percentage must be between 0 and 100\n- `updateProgress` fires all registered `onProgress` callbacks for that task\n- `cancel()` works on both `pending` and `processing` tasks\n- A cancelled pending task is removed from the queue (not available for dequeue)\n- A cancelled processing task has its status set to `'cancelled'`\n- Cancelled tasks are NOT retried (even if they have retries remaining)\n- `getProgress(taskId)` returns `null` if no progress has been reported yet\n- The `message` parameter in `updateProgress` is optional\n- `onProgress` returns an unsubscribe function\n","numLines":23,"startLine":1,"totalLines":23}}}
+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_orig_mnqwy3c7ge3gmmlcmzqwinzqme2gmmlegrqtsobvmeytinq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Phase 10: Recurring Tasks (Cron)\n2\t\n3\tAdd support for recurring scheduled tasks using simple cron-like expressions.\n4\t\n5\t## New Methods\n6\t\n7\t- **`schedule(queueName, cronExpression, taskTemplate)`** - Create a recurring task schedule. Returns a schedule object with an `id`.\n8\t- **`unschedule(scheduleId)`** - Remove a schedule, stopping future occurrences.\n9\t- **`listSchedules(queueName?)`** - List all active schedules, optionally filtered by queue name.\n10\t- **`tick()`** - Manually advance and check schedules, triggering any that are due. For testing purposes.\n11\t\n12\t## Cron Expression Subset\n13\t\n14\tSupport a simple subset of cron expressions:\n15\t\n16\t| Expression       | Meaning              |\n17\t|------------------|----------------------|\n18\t| `*/N * * * *`    | Every N minutes      |\n19\t| `0 * * * *`      | Every hour (on the hour) |\n20\t| `0 0 * * *`      | Every day at midnight |\n21\t\n22\t## Requirements\n23\t\n24\t- `schedule()` returns a schedule object: `{ id, queueName, cronExpression, taskTemplate, active: true }`\n25\t- `tick()` checks all active schedules and enqueues tasks for any that are due\n26\t- The `taskTemplate` is used as the payload for enqueued tasks\n27\t- `unschedule(scheduleId)` marks the schedule as inactive\n28\t- `listSchedules()` returns only active schedules\n29\t- Multiple schedules can coexist on the same or different queues\n30\t- Scheduled tasks are regular tasks once enqueued (subject to all other queue rules)\n31\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bb6276d8-e493-46dd-af72-8571635da619","uuid":"7b3a65f3-a00c-44a3-944b-75fa0463800d","timestamp":"2026-06-18T06:15:49.618Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/phases/phase-10.md","content":"# Phase 10: Recurring Tasks (Cron)\n\nAdd support for recurring scheduled tasks using simple cron-like expressions.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`schedule(queueName, cronExpression, taskTemplate)`** - Create a recurring task schedule. Returns a schedule object with an `id`.\n- **`unschedule(scheduleId)`** - Remove a schedule, stopping future occurrences.\n- **`listSchedules(queueName?)`** - List all active schedules, optionally filtered by queue name.\n- **`tick()`** - Manually advance and check schedules, triggering any that are due. For testing purposes.\n\n## Cron Expression Subset\n\nSupport a simple subset of cron expressions:\n\n| Expression       | Meaning              |\n|------------------|----------------------|\n| `*/N * * * *`    | Every N minutes      |\n| `0 * * * *`      | Every hour (on the hour) |\n| `0 0 * * *`      | Every day at midnight |\n\n## Requirements\n\n- `schedule()` returns a schedule object: `{ id, queueName, cronExpression, taskTemplate, active: true }`\n- `tick()` checks all active schedules and enqueues tasks for any that are due\n- The `taskTemplate` is used as the payload for enqueued tasks\n- `unschedule(scheduleId)` marks the schedule as inactive\n- `listSchedules()` returns only active schedules\n- Multiple schedules can coexist on the same or different queues\n- Scheduled tasks are regular tasks once enqueued (subject to all other queue rules)\n","numLines":31,"startLine":1,"totalLines":31}}}
+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_orig_mnqwy3c7mmytsnzuge3gintgga2deyjygbrwentegmytemq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Phase 11: Middleware Pipeline\n2\t\n3\tAdd a middleware system for queue processing pipelines.\n4\t\n5\t## New Methods\n6\t\n7\t- **`use(queueName, middleware)`** - Add a middleware function to a queue's processing pipeline.\n8\t- **`process(queueName, handler)`** - Register a task handler for a queue and begin processing. The handler runs after all middleware.\n9\t\n10\t## Middleware Signature\n11\t\n12\t```typescript\n13\ttype Middleware = (task: Task, next: () => Promise<void>) => Promise<void>;\n14\t```\n15\t\n16\t## Requirements\n17\t\n18\t- Middleware functions run in the order they were added (first added = first to run)\n19\t- Each middleware must call `next()` to pass control to the next middleware (and eventually the handler)\n20\t- If a middleware does NOT call `next()`, the task processing is skipped\n21\t- Middleware can modify the task object (e.g., add metadata)\n22\t- If a middleware throws an error, the task is marked as failed\n23\t- Each queue has its own independent middleware stack\n24\t- Middleware receives the full task context (task object)\n25\t- `process()` dequeues a task and runs it through the middleware pipeline, then the handler\n26\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bb6276d8-e493-46dd-af72-8571635da619","uuid":"12ae516d-1db0-4300-b822-692ec09f3493","timestamp":"2026-06-18T06:15:49.619Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/phases/phase-11.md","content":"# Phase 11: Middleware Pipeline\n\nAdd a middleware system for queue processing pipelines.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`use(queueName, middleware)`** - Add a middleware function to a queue's processing pipeline.\n- **`process(queueName, handler)`** - Register a task handler for a queue and begin processing. The handler runs after all middleware.\n\n## Middleware Signature\n\n```typescript\ntype Middleware = (task: Task, next: () => Promise<void>) => Promise<void>;\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Middleware functions run in the order they were added (first added = first to run)\n- Each middleware must call `next()` to pass control to the next middleware (and eventually the handler)\n- If a middleware does NOT call `next()`, the task processing is skipped\n- Middleware can modify the task object (e.g., add metadata)\n- If a middleware throws an error, the task is marked as failed\n- Each queue has its own independent middleware stack\n- Middleware receives the full task context (task object)\n- `process()` dequeues a task and runs it through the middleware pipeline, then the handler\n","numLines":26,"startLine":1,"totalLines":26}}}
+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_orig_mnqwy3c7mm2dgmzrhaytsmdcg4ywmzdfmqygeolgha4deoa","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Phase 12: Graceful Shutdown\n2\t\n3\tAdd graceful shutdown capabilities to the task queue.\n4\t\n5\t## New Methods\n6\t\n7\t- **`shutdown(timeout?)`** - Initiate graceful shutdown. Returns a Promise that resolves with a summary.\n8\t  - `timeout`: maximum milliseconds to wait for processing tasks (default: 30000)\n9\t- **`onShutdown(callback)`** - Register a callback to be called during shutdown.\n10\t\n11\t## Requirements\n12\t\n13\t- After `shutdown()` is called, `enqueue()` should throw an error\n14\t- `shutdown()` waits for all currently processing tasks to complete\n15\t- If processing tasks don't complete within the timeout, they are force-cancelled\n16\t- `onShutdown` callbacks are called during the shutdown process\n17\t- The returned summary object includes:\n18\t  - `completed`: number of tasks that completed during shutdown\n19\t  - `cancelled`: number of tasks that were force-cancelled\n20\t  - `elapsed`: time in milliseconds the shutdown took\n21\t- Multiple calls to `shutdown()` should be idempotent (return the same promise/result)\n22\t- If there are no processing tasks, shutdown resolves immediately\n23\t- Force-cancelled tasks have their status set to `'cancelled'`\n24\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bb6276d8-e493-46dd-af72-8571635da619","uuid":"465208a5-228b-44b8-bee5-e6a90c0e7d4c","timestamp":"2026-06-18T06:15:49.616Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/phases/phase-12.md","content":"# Phase 12: Graceful Shutdown\n\nAdd graceful shutdown capabilities to the task queue.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`shutdown(timeout?)`** - Initiate graceful shutdown. Returns a Promise that resolves with a summary.\n  - `timeout`: maximum milliseconds to wait for processing tasks (default: 30000)\n- **`onShutdown(callback)`** - Register a callback to be called during shutdown.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- After `shutdown()` is called, `enqueue()` should throw an error\n- `shutdown()` waits for all currently processing tasks to complete\n- If processing tasks don't complete within the timeout, they are force-cancelled\n- `onShutdown` callbacks are called during the shutdown process\n- The returned summary object includes:\n  - `completed`: number of tasks that completed during shutdown\n  - `cancelled`: number of tasks that were force-cancelled\n  - `elapsed`: time in milliseconds the shutdown took\n- Multiple calls to `shutdown()` should be idempotent (return the same promise/result)\n- If there are no processing tasks, shutdown resolves immediately\n- Force-cancelled tasks have their status set to `'cancelled'`\n","numLines":24,"startLine":1,"totalLines":24}}}
